  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Strong to severe thunderstorms are likely Tuesday, with\n some thunderstorm risk lingering into Wednesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nMoisture is expected to return in earnest tonight in\nstrengthening srly flow. Elevated showers and thunderstorms are\npossible overnight into Tue morning as a front returns\nnorthward. Morning low clouds and a cap will likely delay\nconvective initiation until the mid afternoon, but afternoon\ntemps in the low 90s combined with at least low 70s dewpoints\nshould result in strong instability with 1500-2000 J/kg CAPE\nprogged. This combined with approaching anomalous upper level\ntrough and associated strong wind speeds aloft, particularly for\nthe peak of summer should provide ample forcing and shear to \nresult in a significant severe wx episode Tue. Scattered \nsignificant wind damage reports seem likely, with the potential \nfor isolated tornadoes, and perhaps flash flooding. Flash flood \nthreat does not appear significant enough for a watch given \nanticipated fast storm motions of 30kt or more.\n\nBy Wednesday, there is some uncertainty with the cold frontal \nposition. With the upper trough overhead, additional showers \nand thunderstorms are likely, especially across southern\nMaryland. While some additional strong to severe thunderstorms \ncan't be ruled out if things trend slower, it's beginning to \nappear the best parameters for that to occur will be shunted \ntoward southeast Virginia.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Cooler than average temperatures anticipated \nbeginning Thursday through the end of the week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAn upper-level trough centered over southeastern Canada will track \ntowards the northeast on Thursday, allowing high pressure to slide \ninto the region from the west. This resulting high pressure could \npersist through early Sunday. Dewpoints will also trend lower during \nthis period, resulting in relatively dry conditions. Temperatures \nrange in the low-to-mid 80s, trending slightly below average for \nJuly. This high pressure could begin to break down late this weekend \nas a strong upper-level trough begins to form over the Great Lakes \nregion. As the trailing cold front transitions east, humidity and \nprecipitation chances could begin to increase again at the start of \nnext week as this system moves through.",
